  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...A few spotty showers and thunderstorms through \nthis evening.\n\nAn upper level trough deepens across the Northeast today. An \nembedded shortwave trough is expected to move from the Upper Great \nLakes region to the Ohio Valley. Diurnal heating and modest \ndewpoints across the eastern Great Lakes region will support \nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms inland from lake \nbreezes through early this evening. Further to the west, numerous \nshowers and thunderstorms are expected to develop across southern \nOntario into Ohio this afternoon, and this activity may reach the \neastern Lake Erie shoreline and the Buffalo/Niagara Falls Metros by \nearly evening. Hi-res guidance has trended drier in recent runs. \n\nKey Message 2...Greater coverage of showers and thunderstorms is expected \nfrom Monday through Tuesday night, some of these storms may \nproduce gusty winds and heavy rainfall.\n\nAn upper level ridge will build across the Northern Plains and Upper \nGreat Lakes region through Monday. This will nudge the upper level \ntrough over the Northeast while putting the eastern Great Lakes \nregion within northwest flow aloft. Similar to previous days, \ndiurnal heating and dewpoints in the 60s will support showers and \nthunderstorms inland from lake breezes Monday afternoon. Surface-\nbased CAPE is expected to be higher Monday, leading to an increasing \ncoverage of showers and thunderstorms. Winds aloft remain on the \nweaker side with mean 0-6km wind shear around 15-20 knots across far \nwestern NY, and less than 15 knots elsewhere. A shortwave trough and \nsurface low are expected to dive across the Central Great Lakes to \nthe Lake Erie region through Monday night. A corridor of increasing \nmid-level winds, steepening mid-level lapse rates, and high PWATS \nwill move into the Ohio Valley and parts of western NY Monday night. \nMean 0-6km wind shear will increase to 25-30 knots across far \nwestern NY, remaining less than 20 knots east of the Genesee Valley. \nThe risk for an isolated strong thundertorm increases across \nfar western NY overnight, especially if organized convection \nforms upstream Monday afternoon. If storms develop, the main \nhazards are damaging winds and locallly heavy rainfall. \n\nUpper level troughing will return across the eastern Great Lakes \nregion Tuesday, and the surface low will become stacked and nearly \nstationary across western and central NY. Diurnal heating within a \nvery moist environment will support numerous showers and \nthunderstorms Tuesday. While winds will be weak within the stacked \nlow, a low level jet will wrap around the low and a few strong \nthunderstorms with heavy rainfall may lead to localized heavy \nrainfall across parts of western and central NY Tuesday afternoon \nthrough Tuesday evening. The low pivots into southern New England \nTuesday night with a warm, moist conveyor belt moving into the \neastern Lake Ontario region. The potential for showers and \nthunderstorms with heavy rainfall will continue into this region. \nNortherly flow and wrap-around moisture will move into western NY \nand chances for showers will remain overnight. \n\nWPC has the entire region in a Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall \nMonday through Tuesday night. \n\nKey Message 3...Chances for showers and thunderstorms are expected \nto linger into Thursday, with drier conditions for the end of the \nwork week.\n\nA cool season type set-up will persist Wednesday with a stacked low \nover the Northeast. Deep moisture will persist across the forecast \narea through Wednesday, before drier air builds into the region \nWednesday night through Thursday. Diurnal heating will support \nanother day of showers and thunderstorms across the region. \nCooler weather is expected Wednesday and Thursday with highs in \nthe 70s. \n\nHigh pressure will move into the eastern Great Lakes region for the \nend of the week bringing dry and seasonal weather.",
